WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.740
The first film followed one glucose bond, whose face decides fuel or fiber.

2
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.560
Now look at a red blood cell.

3
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.020
Short sugar chains bristle from its surface.

4
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.620
Change one small sugar at their tips, and it changes which blood you can receive.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.880
How can one sugar matter that much?

6
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:28.203
Most proteins on a cell's surface, and most it secretes, carry chains like these.

7
00:00:29.563 --> 00:00:32.583
Every link in them still runs through an anomeric carbon.

8
00:00:33.803 --> 00:00:36.063
So the first rule tells you where each link is.

9
00:00:37.143 --> 00:00:38.903
It cannot tell you what the coat says.

10
00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:41.907
Your blood type shows what it says.

11
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:46.547
Red cells carry a shared core chain, called the H antigen.

12
00:00:47.787 --> 00:00:51.447
Which sugar lands on its tip depends on the enzyme you inherit.

13
00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:54.607
The enzyme adds one sugar.

14
00:00:55.867 --> 00:00:58.747
The B enzyme adds a different one, by the same bond.

15
00:01:00.047 --> 00:01:01.767
The O allele adds nothing at all.

16
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.810
And the A and B sugars differ at only one carbon, carbon two.

17
00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.770
One small difference at the tip sets your blood type.

18
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:13.393
That is the second rule.

19
00:01:14.673 --> 00:01:19.593
On the cell surface, what a protein reads is which sugar, joined by which bond, at

20
00:01:19.593 --> 00:01:20.593
which position.

21
00:01:21.367 --> 00:01:23.647
The cell writes addresses this way too.

22
00:01:24.947 --> 00:01:28.987
A mannose-6-phosphate tag sends enzymes to the lysosome.

23
00:01:30.207 --> 00:01:34.607
Lose the ability to write that tag, and the enzymes are dumped into the blood

24
00:01:34.607 --> 00:01:37.607
instead. That is I-cell disease.

25
00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.247
A code needs readers.

26
00:01:41.607 --> 00:01:45.747
Here the readers are lectins, proteins that bind specific sugar chains.

27
00:01:47.047 --> 00:01:48.967
But any single sugar grip is weak.

28
00:01:50.017 --> 00:01:53.017
So how can a lectin hold on firmly enough to matter?

29
00:01:54.557 --> 00:01:56.297
Picture an answer before you hear one.

30
00:02:02.233 --> 00:02:04.333
It grips many sugars at once.

31
00:02:05.393 --> 00:02:08.393
Many weak handholds add up to a strong, selective grip.

32
00:02:09.713 --> 00:02:12.593
Think of hook and loop tape: no single hook would hold.

33
00:02:13.617 --> 00:02:16.717
Selectins, one lectin family, use this trick.

34
00:02:17.917 --> 00:02:21.857
Their many grips slow circulating immune cells at a site of injury.

35
00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.130
The flu virus reads the same code.

36
00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:28.250
Its hemagglutinin is a lectin.

37
00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:33.830
It grips sialic acid, the charged sugar capping chains on your airway cells.

38
00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.390
That grip is how the virus latches on before it gets in.

39
00:02:39.383 --> 00:02:43.623
So one small sugar matters because the coat is read like a message.

40
00:02:44.923 --> 00:02:48.503
The readers, ours or a virus's, care which sugar sits where.
